WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:03.600
Why did Mrs.Tomato turn red ?

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:06.960
She saw Mr. Green pea.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:11.880
Watching adventurous movies is always a fun experience.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:16.020
We are always mesmerised by the awesome visual effects.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:17.640
Most of us are aware

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.760
that they are created by using a Green screen

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:21.980
while shooting the movie.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:24.820
But ever wondered how this works?

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.100
And out of all the other colours,

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:30.460
why are Green coloured screens used mostly?

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.720
Can we use other colours?

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:38.820
Let's find out .

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.080
Imagine a person who wants to teach some Astronomy lessons

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.580
by showing fancy stuff in the background.

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:49.400
All she does is add a Green screen in the backdrop.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:51.580
How does this help her?

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.740
Well, the answer lies in the technique called Chroma Keying.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:58.340
It's a process carried out by

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:01.520
various computer application softwares.

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:04.180
What exactly do these softwares do?

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:07.140
Let's understand with our example!

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:11.280
In our video, we want Stars and Planets in the background.

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:14.980
Currently, the whole background of a video is just Green.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.900
So we tell the software to remove the Green colour

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:20.220
from each scene in the video.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:22.920
This makes the background transparent.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:25.120
Now, the job is simple

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:27.740
the software fills the transparent background

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:29.860
with an astronomical scene.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:32.760
The final result we get is the combination

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:37.240
of the character's video footage with the astronomical background.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:40.580
This is the Chroma Keying technique.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.140
It's basically a technique used

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:45.460
to remove a specific colour from an image

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:49.260
and replace it by some other image.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:52.940
Now that we've understood what Chroma Keying is,

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:56.900
can you tell me why the Green colour is specifically used?

00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:59.600
Think about it!

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:04.140
Well, to know that let's understand with a situation.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.340
Here, instead of the Green screen

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:10.520
say we use a Brown screen as the background for our character.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:13.640
Now obviously in the Chroma Keying process,

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:16.600
we will remove the Brown colour from the background.

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:19.000
Notice what just happened?

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:20.060
That's right !

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:22.980
When we remove the Brown colour from the background ,

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:27.240
the same colour tone from our character is also removed.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:31.420
We can now see that the result isn't something what we want .

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:35.900
Alright, what if we take this colour as our background?

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:40.540
Again in the post-production we get an absurd result.

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:43.440
This gives us a hint, doesn't it?

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:45.500
Yes, for the background,

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:47.580
we have to choose some contrasting colours

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:51.020
that do not affect our skin tone or hair colour.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:54.860
And which are those colours that are not contained in our skin tone?

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:57.840
The natural skin tone of the human body

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:00.280
doesn't have Green and Blue colours.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:03.380
The same goes with our natural hair colour.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.380
Do you see any shades of Green and Blue colours?

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:07.680
I doubt it!

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.880
So by default, Green and Blue screens are used in the backdrops.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:17.820
But usually, we prefer Green screens more often than Blue.

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:19.140
Why is that?

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:22.740
The answer lies in the Digital cameras.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:25.800
They are designed in a way we see colours.

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:27.640
What does that mean?

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:30.540
Our eyes are more sensitive to Green colour

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:32.520
than any other colour.

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:34.740
And Digital cameras have sensors

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:37.540
that capture the Green colour accurately.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.300
So, Green being highly luminant

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.180
is easier for computer softwares to identify

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:46.400
and remove during post-production.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:48.540
Another reason is that the Green screen

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:52.160
requires less lighting setup than the Blue screen.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:55.560
Also, subjects are likely to wear Blue outfits

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:57.560
more often than Green ones.

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:01.400
It's very difficult to avoid blue colour outfits.

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:04.740
But what if the person wants to wear Green outfits?

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:10.400
Well, in such rare cases, the Blue screen comes to our rescue .

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:13.880
All right ! Consider this challenging situation.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:18.100
We want to create an action block scene of two characters

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:20.900
with some adventurous background.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:23.920
Here, one character is wearing a Green outfit

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:26.980
while the other one is in a Blue outfit.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:29.220
What should we do here?

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:31.860
Do we have to shoot characters with their respective

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.180
Green and Blue backgrounds separately,

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:37.600
and then merge them together during post-production?

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:40.400
Or can be used both Blue and Green screens

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:42.540
together to shoot the scene?

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:45.440
Or is there any other better way?

